<p>President Trump said he is “not happy” with the pace and substance of U.S.-Iran nuclear negotiations after the latest round of talks in Geneva. Still, he signaled he is not rushing a decision and will let further rounds play out. He reiterated the U.S. position that Iran “cannot have nuclear weapons,” and said military force remains an option even as he stressed he would prefer a diplomatic outcome. Oman, which is mediating the indirect talks, struck a more upbeat tone. Omani officials said the discussions made substantial progress and argued an agreement is “within our reach,” pointing to what they described as a breakthrough idea: Iran would agree to zero stockpiling of uranium and convert existing material into fuel. The mixed messages come as the region takes precautions. The U.S. Embassy in Jerusalem moved to “authorized departure” for non-essential staff and family members, and Secretary of State Marco Rubio is set to visit Israel next week as tensions stay elevated. As diplomacy continues, several governments have issued travel warnings and moved personnel amid fears of escalation—an indication of what’s at stake: avoiding another Middle East war while testing whether a new nuclear understanding is still possible.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/W9qx8RCEW-pzIYtmHjKOI79PwExW_waYcXB8comfiJ4n8RYQco14Nmzu4DCzDCuIcNu9HqvS8pfOYFG3z3Oqlv8DgXduQk9jxjhZADlPK5qbdx9rGIxRHXEVp7ob6QoeiyQ9v-YS2VcfV3d2qmWFw4KCU61HadSJ5gXMs_ATBbDd1ZlzF8o' alt='President Trump addressing reporters before leaving the White House on Feb. 27, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Military posture: PBS reports the U.S. buildup alongside the negotiations has grown into the region’s largest Middle East military presence in more than two decades.</li><li>Israel staffing: U.S. Ambassador to Israel Mike Huckabee urged embassy employees that if they want to leave Israel, they “should do so TODAY,” as the mission shifted to authorized departure.</li><li>Enrichment red line: Trump said he wants Iran to have “no enrichment” of uranium at all—tougher than demands focused on stockpiles alone.</li><li>Inspection pressure: DW says the U.N. nuclear watchdog has urged Tehran to allow inspections at its nuclear sites as deadline pressure and diplomacy intensify.</li><li>War likelihood: CBS News cited experts who say war looks more likely than a deal even as talks resume, underscoring the gap between diplomatic hopes and escalation risks.</li></ul><blockquote>We’re not exactly happy with the way they’re negotiating. They cannot have nuclear weapons. - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Oman (mediator): Oman’s foreign minister said the latest indirect talks made “substantial progress,” arguing a deal is “within our reach.” He pointed to an arrangement in which Iran would accept zero uranium stockpiling and convert existing material into fuel. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/us-iran-deal-within-our-reach-oman-mediator-says/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>U.S. administration: Trump said he wants a deal but insists Iran must not be able to obtain nuclear weapons. He has left military action on the table, while saying he has not made a final decision on strikes. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/27/us/politics/trump-iran-talks.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>United Kingdom: Britain withdrew U.K. diplomatic staff from Iran and said the embassy will operate remotely. The FCDO continues to advise against all travel to Iran due to the security situation. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/uk-diplomatic-staff-withdrawn-from-iran-as-us-considers-strike/'>POLITICO Europe</a>)</li><li>IAEA / U.N. nuclear watchdog: DW reports the watchdog urged Tehran to allow inspections at its nuclear sites as Washington’s strike threat and the diplomatic timetable intensify. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-allows-staff-to-leave-israel-as-trump-attack-on-iran-looms/a-76157922'>DW</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://